1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,333
Movement.

2
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:03,000
So why were you told bulls hate red?

3
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:06,429
Picture a matador, a red cape, a charging bull.

4
00:00:06,429 --> 00:00:11,000
Movies, cartoons, 'red rag to a bull' say color is the trigger.

5
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:12,200
It makes sense:

6
00:00:12,200 --> 00:00:17,000
in the final act the cape is red, and the bull charges.

7
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:21,000
Two things together, so we assumed one caused the other.

8
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:23,947
But earlier, the matador uses another cape:

9
00:00:23,947 --> 00:00:26,474
magenta outside, yellow or blue inside.

10
00:00:26,474 --> 00:00:29,000
Bulls charge it just as readily.

11
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:30,923
Now look at their eyes:

12
00:00:30,923 --> 00:00:34,769
cattle have two kinds of color-detecting cells, not our three.

13
00:00:34,769 --> 00:00:39,000
Red doesn't look to them the way it looks to us.

14
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:40,875
So what sets them off?

15
00:00:40,875 --> 00:00:42,000
Most likely motion.

16
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:48,000
Fighting bulls are bred for aggression, and a cape whipping through the air triggers a charge.

17
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:49,765
It gets weirder.

18
00:00:49,765 --> 00:00:55,059
Around 1600, 'red rag' was slang for the tongue.

19
00:00:55,059 --> 00:00:58,000
The bull part came later.

20
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:02,086
So red is tradition, and it hides stains for the crowd.

21
00:01:02,086 --> 00:01:03,571
The bull never cared.

22
00:01:03,571 --> 00:01:10,257
Next time someone says red drives bulls wild, tell them what sets a bull off isn't a color.

23
00:01:10,257 --> 00:01:11,000
It's the
